cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this apparatus, please cite it as below. When citing a passage,
  cite the printed original — the paragraph grids exist so that you can;
  where a numbering or a working translation is this site's own (stated per
  module), name the site as its source.
title: "Psychological Predecessors of AI"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Fassbender
    given-names: Pantaleon
abstract: >-
  A digital research apparatus for the psychological prehistory of
  artificial intelligence: the public-domain sources, 1749–1930, in which
  the mind became mechanism, measure, and law of learning. Four lines — the
  measured mind (Fechner, Ebbinghaus, Galton, Spearman, Binet), the
  learning animal (Hartley, Bain, Morgan, Loeb, Thorndike, Watson, Pavlov,
  Köhler), the automaton debate (Huxley, James, McDougall) and the machine
  in the laboratory (Hull) — in paragraph-exact citable editions, with a
  cross-corpus concordance, a concept atlas, a chronological timeline,
  plates from the cited digitisations, and an introductory essay. The
  corpus ends, deliberately, in 1930 — where the machines begin to learn.
  Sister apparatus to Calculemus: Philosophical Predecessors of AI
  (doi:10.5281/zenodo.22677079).
keywords:
  - history of psychology
  - history of artificial intelligence
  - digital humanities
  - public domain editions
  - psychophysics
  - intelligence testing
  - law of effect
  - comparative psychology
  - distant writing
